      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "1. Evaluate Current MFA Setup\n- Identify the different services used by employees, both internally (i.e. law firm CRM, file storage, accounting) and externally (i.e. billable rates driven by personal email accounts).\n- Determine the percentage of users, based on roles and access needs, who are currently using each MFA tool.\n\n2. Define MPA Policy\n- The MPA policy should outline criteria for personnel that fall under the MPA policy, and procedures and consequences for both violators and staff supporting them in their roles.\n- Policy should be comprehensive in addressing situations like trying to expedite processes, bank fraud, security investigations, etc.\n- Make sure that your employees understand the necessity of MFA and the pros and cons of the different types of MFA (e.g., SMS, TOTP, MFA Wallet, security keys, etc.) and the reason for moving from SMS to security keys\n\n3. Plan Deployment\n- Roll out MFA devices that will be placed physically within the law firm premises to avoid cloud or physical access vulnerabilities temporarily until you are secure (i.e., lost or damaged devices).\n- Set up a team (i.e. trained IT staff or small dedicated team) to coordinate MFA deployment in new contracts or cases to avoid",
